WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.870
INTRO:
Android is one of the most popular operating

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:08.590
systems in the entire world with an active
user base of 2.5 billion people.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:13.660
Many people swear by Android and would never
even consider iOS thanks to Android’s versatility

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.000
and freedom.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.040
Considering this popularity and strong fanbase,
you would think that Android’s founder is

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.690
a business icon like Bill Gates or Steve Jobs,
but this is not exactly the case.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:30.039
In fact, there’s a large group of people
who despise the founder of Android, Andy Rubin.

00:00:30.039 --> 00:00:35.540
Google actually paid Andy $90 million to leave
the company in October of 2014.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:40.410
And when the details regarding his resignation
from the company became public, 20,000 Google

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.930
employees participated in a walkout protest.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:47.969
Things actually got so heated that many shareholders
filed lawsuits against Google, and Google

00:00:47.969 --> 00:00:51.790
would end up settling for $310 million in
2020.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:56.039
From Google’s perspective, there’s no
doubt that this $400 million along with the

00:00:56.039 --> 00:00:59.870
estimated $50 million they paid to acquire
Android were more than worth it.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:04.080
But the same cannot be said about Andy given
that he not only missed out on billions of

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.179
dollars by selling to Google but also lost
his reputation.

00:01:07.179 --> 00:01:12.680
So, here’s what happened to Android’s
founder.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:19.139
PRE ANDROID:
Taking a look back, Andy Rubin was born in

00:01:19.139 --> 00:01:21.349
1963 in Chappaqua, New York.

00:01:21.349 --> 00:01:25.899
By trade, his father was a psychologist, but
by the time Andy grew up, his father would

00:01:25.899 --> 00:01:28.420
embark on his own entrepreneurial journey.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:32.670
He opened up a marketing firm that took pictures
of the latest electronic gadgets.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:38.069
This gave Andy unprecedented access to newly
released or even pre-released tech, and this

00:01:38.069 --> 00:01:41.069
is what really sparked his interest in technology.

00:01:41.069 --> 00:01:44.479
Andy would end up attending the local high
school in his city called Horace Greeley High

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:47.319
School and he would graduate in 1981.

00:01:47.319 --> 00:01:51.869
Following his primary education, Andy would
enroll in Utica College and major in computer

00:01:51.869 --> 00:01:53.219
science.

00:01:53.219 --> 00:01:56.959
Despite this background in software, Andy
didn’t instantly pick up a coding job at

00:01:56.959 --> 00:01:58.189
a tech firm.

00:01:58.189 --> 00:02:02.570
His first job out of college was at Carl Zeiss
X-ray Microscopy where he primarily worked

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:03.920
with robotics.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.020
It seems that early on in his career, Andy
didn’t have larger-than-life ambitions.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:11.750
He simply wanted to work on robotics and see
the world.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:12.750
This desire led him to Geneva Switzerland
where he got a job at a Swiss firm as a software

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:13.750
engineer.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.680
This job didn’t last long though as Andy
would soon take a vacation that would change

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.849
the trajectory of his life.

00:02:17.849 --> 00:02:22.659
After a year in Geneva, Andy decided to take
a trip to the Cayman Islands in 1989 where

00:02:22.659 --> 00:02:25.909
he would run into an Apple engineer named
Bill Caswell.

00:02:25.909 --> 00:02:29.730
Bill and Andy quickly became friends thanks
to their shared interest in engineering, and

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:32.599
Bill would end up offering Andy a job at Apple.

00:02:32.599 --> 00:02:37.170
Andy of course took the job and would end
up as a manufacturing engineer at Apple.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.880
This is actually where Andy would earn the
nickname of “Android” from his coworkers

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:44.879
due to his obsession with robotics, but it
wasn’t all sunshine and rainbows.

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:48.930
Apple as a whole wasn’t doing all that well
during this time period.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:53.359
Steve Jobs had been fired years before this
and Apple was slowly bleeding out.

00:02:53.359 --> 00:02:58.560
So, in 1992, Andy switched over to a spinoff
of Apple called General Magic that seemed

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.489
to have better potential.

00:03:00.489 --> 00:03:04.819
Most of us have probably never heard of General
Magic given that the company shut down 20

00:03:04.819 --> 00:03:05.819
years ago.

00:03:05.819 --> 00:03:10.150
But, within the tech world, General Magic
is often credited as being the most important

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:12.390
dead company in Silicon Valley.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:16.939
They worked on several groundbreaking technologies
including smartphones, mobile operating systems,

00:03:16.939 --> 00:03:19.370
e-commerce, social media, and even emojis.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:23.540
There’s even a full-on documentary on General
Magic if you’re interested.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:28.260
But, while they had game-changing ideas, these
visions never turned into tangible success.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:33.079
And Andy would switch to WebTV in 1995 and
become a communications engineer.

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:37.950
WebTV focused on making the internet accessible
through TVs and would be acquired by Microsoft

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:42.790
for $425 million in 1997 and turn into MSN
TV.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:47.570
Andy stuck around for a few years after the
acquisition, but in 1999, he would decide

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:50.950
that he was done with jumping from one tech
company to another.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:56.239
He wanted to work on something much larger,
so in 1999 he would quit his job at Microsoft

00:03:56.239 --> 00:03:58.290
and found his first company.

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:03.920
FOUNDING ANDROID:
Andy’s first company wasn’t Android but

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.249
actually a company called Danger Inc.

00:04:06.249 --> 00:04:10.909
Andy co-founded this company with two of his
friends including Joe Britt and Matt Hershenson.

00:04:10.909 --> 00:04:15.750
The company focused on creating software and
services for mobile devices, and one of their

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.519
first major partnerships was with T-mobile.

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:23.240
If you were born in the early 1990s, I’m
sure you remember the Tmobile sidekick.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.169
It was that phone that you could slide open
and type on using a full keyboard.

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:31.590
Danger was actually the company that wrote
all the software for this device, and they

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:35.150
also played a significant role in the design
of the phone as well.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:39.500
The default search engine used on these devices
was Google.com and this would catch the attention

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.090
of Larry Page.

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:45.490
Larry and Andy would end up meeting in 2002
at a Stanford event, and this would be the

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:47.949
beginning of a decade-long relationship.

00:04:47.949 --> 00:04:52.199
Danger would go on to release several iterations
of the sidekick, but Andy wanted to focus

00:04:52.199 --> 00:04:53.910
on the software side of Danger.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:59.000
So, in 2003, he started a side project called
Android with a few others.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.960
The original goal of the project was to create
an open-source operating system for digital

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.310
cameras, but they quickly switched over to
cell phones.

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:10.509
Android was initially just a side project
that Andy worked on during the winter, but

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:14.160
in 2004, he would leave Danger and go all-in
on Android.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:19.270
By this point, Larry had known Andy for a
few years and could easily see the potential

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:20.270
of Android.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:24.320
So, very early on in the project, Larry offered
to acquire Android.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:29.110
It was never revealed how much exactly Google
paid for Android, but given the total amount

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:34.440
Google spent on acquisitions in 2005, it’s
estimated that they only paid $50 million.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.539
This is quite ironic given that Microsoft
would end up acquiring Danger for ten times

00:05:38.539 --> 00:05:40.410
that amount just a few years later.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:46.360
It’s not clear if Andy held onto his stake
in Danger or not, but sticking at Danger would’ve

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:48.259
been the better financial move in the short
term.

00:05:48.259 --> 00:05:53.039
In the long term though, there’s no doubt
that switching to Android was the right move.

00:05:53.039 --> 00:05:56.800
Once Android was acquired by Google, Andy
no longer had to worry about funding issues

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.130
or stability as Google could easily support
the operation.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:04.000
So, he focused on trying to create an operating
system for a phone with a physical keyboard.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.889
But, once the iPhone launched in 2007, it
was clear that Android had to target the smartphone

00:06:08.889 --> 00:06:09.889
market.

00:06:09.889 --> 00:06:13.520
The problem though was that they weren’t
the only large players with such ambitions.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:20.199
HTC, Motorola, Samsung, Sprint, T-Mobile,
Qualcomm, and TI all wanted to create their

00:06:20.199 --> 00:06:22.780
own open-source operating system for smartphones.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:26.979
But, Android had a multi-year development
lead on all the competition.

00:06:26.979 --> 00:06:31.080
And with the Samsung Galaxy and the Google
Nexus embracing Android in 2010, there was

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:35.699
little doubt that Google had probably won
the mobile OS race, and Andy Rubin quickly

00:06:35.699 --> 00:06:39.670
became one of the most important executives
at Google.

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:44.690
DEPARTING ANDROID:
Things were looking phenomenal for Andy.

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:48.940
He had more than proved his worth and Android
has since become known as Google’s best

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:50.979
acquisition of all time.

00:06:50.979 --> 00:06:55.520
Personally, I’d have to disagree with this
statement as I think YouTube is Google’s

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:00.340
best acquisition, but there’s no doubt that
Android is definitely within the top 3.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:05.800
But, despite all this positive momentum, in
October of 2014, Google would drop a bombshell.

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:10.319
They announced that Andy Rubin would be leaving
Google altogether to start up his own hardware

00:07:10.319 --> 00:07:11.419
company.

00:07:11.419 --> 00:07:15.810
This was completely unexpected not only to
the tech world but even to most of Andy’s

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:17.530
coworkers within Google.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:21.910
None of them had expected Andy to leave Google
so abruptly given that he seemed pretty content

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:23.160
with his role there.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:26.250
Speculation surrounding Andy’s departure
became rampant.

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:28.280
Did Andy get bored with Android?

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:30.020
Did Andy and Larry have a conflict?

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:34.169
Does Andy not like the direction in which
Google was taking Android and so on and so

00:07:34.169 --> 00:07:35.310
forth.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:39.919
Despite all these questions, both Andy and
Google remained pretty quiet about the situation

00:07:39.919 --> 00:07:44.009
and after a few months, the media moved onto
the next piece of drama within the tech world.

00:07:44.009 --> 00:07:48.020
It wasn’t till four years later that the
New York Times uncovered what truly unfolded.

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:52.759
According to the New York times, Google had
received a complaint from at least 1 Google

00:07:52.759 --> 00:07:56.139
employee that Andy was involved in misconduct
and harassment.

00:07:56.139 --> 00:08:00.830
Google apparently investigated the allegations
and determined that they were in fact credible.

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:04.030
It was clear to Google’s executives that
they could no longer associate themselves

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:05.030
with Andy.

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:09.629
But, at the same time, Andy had played such
an influential role at the company that they

00:08:09.629 --> 00:08:11.229
didn’t want to just fire him.

00:08:11.229 --> 00:08:15.289
So, they basically told Andy, Hey Andy, we
know what you did.

00:08:15.289 --> 00:08:18.879
We’re not gonna escalate this to the authorities
or oust you publicly.

00:08:18.879 --> 00:08:21.569
But, we also can’t have you stay here.

00:08:21.569 --> 00:08:24.669
So, just take this $90 million and leave.

00:08:24.669 --> 00:08:28.539
Andy of course denies all of this and claims
that his departure from Google was 100% amicable.

00:08:28.539 --> 00:08:34.400
And technically, these allegations were never
confirmed in court, so there is no verdict

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:35.620
on Andy’s guilt.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:39.830
Nonetheless, when the news broke, people around
the world and especially Google employees

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:41.070
were livid.

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:44.830
Many felt that Google should have fired Andy
on the spot and reported him to the authorities

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:47.680
instead of sending him off with $90 million.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:51.030
And tens of thousands of these guys would
stage a walkout protest against Google in

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:52.560
late 2018.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.610
As we touched on at the beginning of the video,
these protests would escalate into lawsuits

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:59.380
against Google that would stretch out for
years.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:03.560
Google would apologize for the mishandling
of past cases and confirmed that no future

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:07.600
employee that was let go for misconduct allegations
would be eligible for a severance package.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:12.910
They also agreed to invest $310 million into
new diversity, equality, and inclusion measures

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:14.930
that would prevent such situations from reoccurring.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:20.050
And with that, Google had completely washed
their hands of Andy Rubin, but for Andy, it

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:21.050
wasn’t nearly that easy.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:27.750
ANDY RUBIN TODAY:
After leaving Google, Andy co-founded a venture

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:29.830
capital firm called Playground Global.

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:34.370
Thanks to all of the team’s connections
within the tech world, in 2015, they were

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:39.810
able to raise $300 million in venture capital
from investors including Google, HP, Foxconn,

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:40.810
and Tencent.

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:44.270
They turned around and invested this money
into a bunch of tech startups with the most

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:46.430
popular one being Owl Labs.

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:50.840
Andy focused on Playground Global for a few
years, but soon enough, he returned to his

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:53.380
roots and founded a smartphone start-up called
Essential Products.

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:57.930
The company never blew up like Android or
Danger, but it did enjoy a decent level of

00:09:57.930 --> 00:09:58.930
success.

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:03.960
However, this would basically evaporate overnight
once the allegations about Andy came out.

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:08.910
The company would hang on for a few years,
but they eventually shut down in early 2020.

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:12.270
Andy also left Playground Global around the
same time in late 2019.

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:17.070
You would think that with all these allegations
and negative press, Andy would play it safe

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:18.070
and stay out of the limelight.

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:22.130
But, in 2019, he would make headline news
again.

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:26.220
This time, he didn’t necessarily do anything
illegal, but he did do something that was

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:27.740
arguably immoral.

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:31.880
Apparently, Andy made his wife sign a prenup
two weeks before she gave birth to their child.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:35.810
She has since filed a lawsuit against Andy
accusing him of coercing and fraudulently

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:40.400
inducing her to sign the prenup, but this
case hasn’t really gone anywhere.

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:44.390
Since then, Andy has basically disassociated
himself from all the ventures he was involved

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:46.390
in and has gone dead silent.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:51.370
I guess it doesn’t really matter for him
given that he’s worth $350 million, but

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:54.560
his current situation is truly quite a shame.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:57.360
Andy Rubin has ended up as the man who has
everything...

00:10:57.360 --> 00:10:58.360
and nothing.

